{
  "dom_id": "step-q140310",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+\\frac{5}{4}$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{5}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{\u23df}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11.\n$$",
